conceive_without_mind	one
conceive_unconceived	two	unconceived	P3	conceiving objects whose represented situation is one in which no one perceives or thinks of them (what the thought is about)	conceiving objects while they are, in fact, not being conceived by anyone, the conceiver included (which would contradict the act of conceiving)
# P2 needs only the first reading: to conceive that objects may exist without the mind, the scenario you imagine must contain no perceiver.
# P3 calls the claim a "manifest repugnancy," and that holds only on the second reading, since you are conceiving the objects at that moment.
# Both uses come from the same sentence (S5), so this split is a reading of S5 rather than an explicit shift in the text. I am fairly but not fully confident in it.
